\begin{IDEA}
**Title: Estimating Baryon Fraction Scaling via Velocity-Weighted Cross-Correlation**

To overcome the sub-pixel SNR of ~0.002, this study replaces direct stacking with a velocity-weighted cross-correlation between the observed map and a synthetic template constructed from the halo catalog. We will define a signal estimator $S = \sum_i (T_{obs, i} \cdot \hat{v}_{i, los})$, where the template utilizes the known peculiar velocities $v_{los}$ to isolate the kSZ signal from the uncorrelated CMB and white noise components. By partitioning the 5,000 halos into mass bins, we will measure the correlation amplitude, which is directly proportional to the product $\langle \tau \rangle \langle v^2 \rangle$. By isolating the velocity-weighted signal, we mitigate the CMB variance—which acts as additive noise in the correlation—to quantify the deviation of the inferred Thomson optical depth from the theoretical $M^{2/3}$ scaling. This approach effectively converts the 60 $\mu$K map noise into a statistical uncertainty on the global gas-mass fraction, providing a robust measurement of cluster baryon content that is independent of primary CMB fluctuations.
\end{IDEA}


